<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=vulcanization">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vulcanization</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Vulcanisation, Curing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vulcanization is the hardening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, originally of natural rubber treated with sulfur and heat, which forms cross-links between its polymer chains and makes it firmer and more durable. Ancient Mesoamerican cultures already cured rubber with sulfur-rich plant juices, an early form of the process. Charles Goodyear, who worked in the 1830s to strengthen rubber, found in 1839 that a mixture of rubber and sulfur dropped on a hot pan hardened instead of melting, and by 1844 he had patented the process and produced the rubber on an industrial scale. Thomas Hancock had taken out a British patent eight weeks earlier, in November 1843.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vulcanization.jpg" alt="Vulcanization" width="330" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfred T. Palmer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VulcanizationMold1941.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Vulcanization is a range of processes for hardening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubbers</a>. The term first referred only to the treatment of natural rubber with sulfur and heat, which remains the most common practice, but it has grown to cover synthetic rubbers hardened by other means, such as silicone rubber cured at room temperature and neoprene cured with metal oxides. It works by forming cross-links between sections of the polymer chain, which increases rigidity and durability and changes mechanical and electrical properties. Like the curing of other thermosetting polymers, it is generally irreversible.</p><p>In sulfur vulcanization, some carbon-hydrogen bonds next to double bonds are replaced by chains of sulfur atoms that link one polymer chain to another. The number of sulfur atoms in a bridge affects the finished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>: short cross-links give better heat resistance, while longer ones give good dynamic properties but less heat resistance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The word was suggested by William Brockedon, a friend of Thomas Hancock, who obtained the British patent, and refers to the god Vulcan, associated with heat and sulfur in volcanoes. In ancient Mesoamerican cultures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> was made into balls, sandal soles, elastic bands and waterproof containers, and was cured with sulfur-rich plant juices.</p><p>In the 1830s Charles Goodyear tried to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> tires that did not turn soft and sticky in heat. His announced formulas repeatedly failed because the hardening he saw came from the heating itself. In 1839 he dropped a mixture of rubber and sulfur into a hot frying pan, and the rubber stayed firm and hardened further as the heat rose. Hancock took out a British patent on 21 November 1843, eight weeks before Goodyear's American patent of 30 January 1844. Accounts differ on whether Hancock had examined samples of Goodyear's rubber.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> products are vulcanised, which greatly improves their lifespan, function and strength. Examples include hoses, shoe soles, toys, erasers, shock absorbers, conveyor belts and bowling balls, and above all tires, for which sulfur vulcanization of natural rubber and styrene-butadiene rubber is used.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vulcanization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
